      "title": "Software Delivery & Operations in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand software pipelines, containers, monitoring, logs, and secrets, with clear distinctions between technical capabilities and verified Iranian adoption."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "from-code-to-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From source code to a working service",
        "content": "A software product has an operational life beyond the moment its code is written. Changes must be assembled, checked, delivered, and observed after release. Continuous integration and continuous delivery, usually abbreviated CI/CD, organize part of that journey into repeatable automated work. GitLab's documentation describes pipelines whose jobs can build applications, run tests, and deploy code. These are capabilities of the tooling; they do not establish how widely Iranian organizations use it. [s1]\n\nThis distinction matters when exploring Iran's technology businesses. A product demonstration can establish that a pipeline runs, while a claim about national adoption requires evidence from organizations or a representative survey. The sources inspected for this page support an explanation of the infrastructure, rather than a ranking of Iranian operators or an estimate of their market size.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "distinct-operational-layers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different tools answer different questions",
        "content": "Pipeline tools organize work around a change: what should run, in what order, and under which conditions? GitLab Runner supplies the execution side, receiving jobs and reporting their results. A repository server and the machines executing its jobs therefore have different responsibilities, even when one organization operates both. [s1][s2]\n\nContainer management addresses the running application. Kubernetes coordinates containerized workloads through declared configurations and automation. It does not replace every surrounding system: its overview explicitly distinguishes orchestration from building source code, prescribing a complete monitoring stack, or providing every application-level service. A release pipeline and a workload platform are complementary layers. [s3]\n\nMonitoring asks whether a service is behaving acceptably. Google's site reliability engineering guidance distinguishes measurements taken inside a system from checks of its externally visible behavior. It emphasizes latency, traffic, errors, and saturation as useful signals. Logs supply event detail that can help explain a symptom, but collecting more records is not the same as establishing that users can complete a task. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "delivery-map",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified delivery and feedback loop",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Source change\"] -->|\"Triggers configured work\"| B[\"Pipeline jobs\"]\n  B -->|\"Execute on\"| C[\"Runner infrastructure\"]\n  C -->|\"Produces\"| D[\"Build output\"]\n  D -->|\"Deployment job delivers\"| E[\"Running service\"]\n  E -->|\"Exposes behavior\"| F[\"Monitoring and logs\"]\n  F -->|\"Informs investigation\"| A\n```",
        "summary": "This editorial synthesis shows how automated execution and operational feedback can connect. It is a conceptual workflow, not a documented architecture of an Iranian organization or a claim that every deployment follows this sequence.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "security-and-responsibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Automation creates responsibilities",
        "content": "Delivery infrastructure handles powerful permissions. GitLab warns that runners execute repository-defined code and can expose their hosts, other projects, or credentials if isolation is inadequate. The security boundary therefore extends beyond the application being built to the build environment itself. [s5]\n\nSecrets management is another separate discipline. Kubernetes provides Secret objects for confidential values, but base64 encoding is not encryption. Its guidance calls for encryption at rest and restricted access. Merely placing a password in a platform's designated object does not demonstrate that the surrounding permissions and storage are secure. [s6]\n\nTogether, these distinctions explain the branches of this guide: pipelines automate change; containers organize execution; monitoring and logs support understanding; secrets management protects access; and DevOps services help organizations integrate those responsibilities. They also provide concrete subjects for assessing a service provider: demonstrable execution, understandable failure handling, controlled credentials, and evidence of operating the resulting system. These are evaluation questions derived from the documented responsibilities, rather than verified claims about any Iranian supplier. [s1][s3][s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
