    "description": "Kubernetes manages containerized applications by coordinating workloads across a cluster of machines. Its architecture separates decisions about desired application state from the node-level work of running containers.",
    "tags": ["Kubernetes", "Containers", "Orchestration", "Cloud infrastructure", "Platform operations", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:17:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:17:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Kubernetes & Container Management in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Kubernetes clusters, Pods, controlled rollouts, and security responsibilities, with explicit limits on evidence about Iranian adoption."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "container-and-cluster",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Packaging an application and coordinating its execution",
        "content": "Containers package applications with their runtime dependencies. Kubernetes supplies a platform for coordinating containerized workloads rather than requiring an operator to manage each running instance individually. Its central idea is declarative configuration: an operator describes a desired arrangement, and the system works toward that state. This does not make Kubernetes a complete application platform or remove the need for surrounding operational systems. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between packaging and orchestration is useful when reading descriptions of Iranian cloud services. A service that can run a container has not thereby demonstrated a particular Kubernetes architecture, managed-service responsibility model, or reliability outcome. Those require evidence about the actual offering. This page explains the underlying technology without attributing unverified capabilities to local providers.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cluster-responsibilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Inside a cluster",
        "content": "A cluster separates control-plane functions from worker-node functions. The API server exposes the Kubernetes interface; etcd stores API-server data; the scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes; and controllers implement the system's reconciliation behavior. On a node, the kubelet helps ensure that the specified Pods and their containers run, using a container runtime. A Pod is Kubernetes' smallest deployable workload unit. [s2][s3]\n\nThese components have different operational roles. Scheduling a workload is distinct from executing its containers, and retaining cluster configuration is distinct from preserving application data. The architecture therefore explains why a working application endpoint alone cannot establish the health or recoverability of every underlying component. That is an operational inference from the documented division of responsibilities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "desired-state",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How a Deployment becomes running application instances",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Operator\"] -->|\"Submits desired configuration\"| B[\"Kubernetes API\"]\n  B -->|\"Exposes Deployment state\"| C[\"Deployment controller\"]\n  C -->|\"Manages\"| D[\"ReplicaSet\"]\n  D -->|\"Maintains desired Pod count\"| E[\"Pods\"]\n  F[\"Scheduler\"] -->|\"Assigns to suitable nodes\"| E\n  G[\"Node kubelet and runtime\"] -->|\"Run specified containers\"| E\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows distinguish configuration, controller ownership, scheduling, and execution. This is a simplified explanation of documented Kubernetes behavior, not a diagram of an Iranian provider's infrastructure.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rollouts-and-secrets",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Controlled change still needs operational judgment",
        "content": "A Deployment manages a set of Pods, commonly for an application that does not maintain state. Updating its Pod template creates a new ReplicaSet and allows a controlled transition between revisions. Kubernetes also provides rollout status and rollback mechanisms. A completed rollout establishes defined workload conditions; it does not independently verify every business function of the application. [s4]\n\nConfidential configuration needs separate attention. Kubernetes Secrets can hold passwords, tokens, and keys, but their values are encoded rather than automatically encrypted in storage. The project recommends encryption at rest and narrowly granted access. Its guidance also explains that permission to create a Pod using a Secret can expose the Secret indirectly. Security therefore depends on effective permissions as well as object names. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persian-resources",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian documentation and the limits of local evidence",
        "content": "The Kubernetes project provides a Persian-language documentation portal, with translated material and links to English sections. This is a concrete language-access resource, but its existence does not measure Iranian deployment numbers or demonstrate that every technical section is translated. [s6]\n\nFor an Iran-focused account, the next evidentiary step is documented implementation: who operates the control plane, which responsibilities remain with customers, and what independent evidence establishes service behavior. Until those details are verified, national adoption rates, provider comparisons, and claims of regional leadership would go beyond this source set.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      }
    ],
